<h2 id="two-series">Two separate series</h2>

<p>The basic State Pension and the new State Pension are different things. The new State Pension began on <span class="date">6 April 2016</span>, so it has no rate before {{ (history.years | where("tax_year", "2016/17") | first).tax_year }}. This page never joins the two into one line, because no person's pension moved from one to the other.</p>

<p>Both are full rates, before tax. They are not what each person received: see <a href="/state-pension/#what-people-receive">what people actually receive</a>.</p>

<h2 id="decisions">Every rate and decision</h2>

<p>Prices are the rise in the Consumer Prices Index (CPI), the measure of inflation the triple lock uses. The increase was decided with the Retail Prices Index (RPI), an older measure of prices, in 2010/11, before the triple lock, and in 2011/12, its first year. The Prices column shows the CPI from 2011/12.</p>

{% call m.scrollTable() %}
<table>
  <caption id="table-rates-decisions">Full weekly rates and the increase applied each year, as published</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Tax year</th><th scope="col" class="num">Basic State Pension</th><th scope="col" class="num">New State Pension</th><th scope="col" class="num">Increase applied</th><th scope="col" class="num">Prices</th><th scope="col" class="num">Earnings, at the time</th><th scope="col">Took effect</th><th scope="col">Decided by</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {%- for year in history.years %}
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">{{ year.tax_year }}{% if year.exceptional %} <span class="status">Exceptional</span>{% endif %}</th>
      <td class="num">{{ year.basic_pence | money }}</td>
      <td class="num">{% if year.new_pence %}{{ year.new_pence | money }}{% else %}Did not exist{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ year.applied_tenths | percent }}</td>
      <td class="num">{% if year.prices_tenths !== null %}{{ year.prices_tenths | percent }}{% else %}Not a test that year{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="num">{% if year.earnings_tenths !== null %}{{ year.earnings_tenths | percent }}{% if year.earnings_not_used %} (not used){% endif %}{% else %}Not a test that year{% endif %}</td>
      <td>{{ year.effective_date | dateTag | safe }}</td>
      <td>{{ { "prices": "Prices (CPI)", "earnings": "Earnings", "floor": "The 2.5% minimum", "rpi": "Prices (RPI, this year only)", "pre-triple-lock": "The higher of 2.5% or RPI" }[year.measure] }}</td>
    </tr>
    {%- endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endcall %}
<div class="source">
  <p><span class="status">Published rate</span> Great Britain. Prices are for the year to the September before. Earnings are for May to July of the year before, as published at the time of the decision. In 2022/23 the earnings figure was not used. In 2016/17 the basic State Pension rose on 11 April and the new State Pension began on 6 April.</p>
  <p>Sources: <a href="{{ history.sources.rates_url }}">{{ history.sources.rates }}</a> <a href="{{ history.sources.decisions_url }}">{{ history.sources.decisions }}</a> Checked against the sources on {{ history.checked_date | dateTag | safe }}.</p>
</div>

<p>Each rate follows from the one before, the increase applied, and rounding to the nearest 5p. We tested this for every increase from 2011/12, and for the new State Pension from 2017/18. {{ m.c("rounding-5p") }}</p>

<h2 id="exceptions">The exceptional years</h2>

<dl>
  {%- for year in history.years %}{% if year.exceptional %}
  <dt>{{ year.tax_year }}</dt>
  <dd>{{ year.note }}</dd>
  {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
</dl>
<p>{{ m.c("tl-2011-rpi") }} {{ m.c("tl-2021-act") }} {{ m.c("tl-2022-act") }}</p>

<p>Because of these years, applying today's rule to every past year does not reproduce what was paid. The published decision is the record.</p>

<h2 id="revisions">Why the earnings figures differ from those published now</h2>

<p>The Office for National Statistics revises its earnings figures. The figure in a decision is the one published at the time.
{% set changed = 0 %}{% set counted = 0 %}{% for year in history.years %}{% if year.earnings_tenths !== null %}{% set counted = counted + 1 %}{% if year.earnings_tenths != year.earnings_current_tenths %}{% set changed = changed + 1 %}{% endif %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
In {{ changed }} of the {{ counted }} years from 2011/12, the figure published now is different.</p>

{% call m.scrollTable() %}
<table>
  <caption id="table-earnings-revisions">Earnings growth: the figure at the time of each decision, and the figure published now</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">For tax year</th><th scope="col" class="num">At the time of the decision</th><th scope="col" class="num">Published now</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    {%- for year in history.years %}{% if year.earnings_tenths !== null %}
    <tr><th scope="row">{{ year.tax_year }}</th><td class="num">{{ year.earnings_tenths | percent }}{% if year.earnings_not_used %} (not used){% endif %}</td><td class="num">{{ year.earnings_current_tenths | percent }}</td></tr>
    {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endcall %}
<div class="source">
  <p><span class="status">Observed statistic</span> <span class="status">Can be revised</span> Average weekly earnings, whole economy, total pay including bonuses, May to July compared with a year earlier.</p>
  <p>Source for the figures published now: <a href="{{ history.sources.earnings_current_url }}">{{ history.sources.earnings_current }}</a> Checked against the source on {{ history.checked_date | dateTag | safe }}.</p>
</div>

<p>The calculations on this page use the figures as they stood at each decision. A calculation from the figures published now would give slightly different results.</p>

<h2 id="explorer">The history explorer</h2>

<p>The <a href="/tools/history/">history explorer</a> draws these rates as a chart and table, in cash, in buying power or against earnings, and can calculate what another rule would have produced from a year you choose.</p>

<h2 id="download">Download the data</h2>

<p><a href="/assets/data/state-pension-full-rates.csv" download>Download the published rates and decisions for both pensions (CSV)</a>. The file holds the published figures only, in cash, with the notes for exceptional years. It has no calculated values.</p>

<p>The figures are compiled by this site from official publications. This site is not an official statistics producer.</p>
